query_text:
  Original workflow request: crea un workflow para coger los emails que vaya recibiendo y que los clasifique por nivel de urgencia con IA
  Workflow objective: User requested a new workflow for: crea un workflow para coger los emails que vaya recibiendo y que los clasifique por nivel de urgencia con IA
  Desired outcome: Deliver an abstract workflow plan that satisfies: crea un workflow para coger los emails que vaya recibiendo y que los clasifique por nivel de urgencia con IA
  Workflow summary: Abstract workflow plan for receiving emails from Gmail, classifying them by urgency (low, medium, high, critical) using AI, and applying the classification result directly to the original email in Gmail without additional storage or notifications.
  Current stage id: stage_3
  Current stage name: Apply Urgency Label to Original Email in Gmail
  Stage purpose: Take the classification result from the previous stage and apply it as a visible label directly to the original email in Gmail.
  Stage required capabilities: Apply a label (e.g., 'urgency-low', 'urgency-medium', etc.) directly to the original email in Gmail., Ensure that the label is visible and persistent, allowing users to filter or search by it in Gmail’s UI., Handle cases where applying the label fails (e.g., due to Gmail API limits); in such cases, log the failure internally but do not stop processing.
  Stage expected inputs: The original email content and metadata from stage 1., A classification result (low, medium, high, critical, or unclassified) from stage 2.
  Stage expected outputs: The original email now labeled in Gmail with the appropriate urgency label., If application fails, an internal log entry noting the failure and the reason.
  Stage dependencies: stage_1, stage_2
  Previous stage node selections: stage_1: n8n-nodes-base.gmailTrigger | stage_2: n8n-nodes-base.openAi
  Clarifications already provided:
  -
  Retrieval focus hint: Prefer nodes that mutate the source item directly, such as adding labels or tags, moving folders, archiving, or updating source-side state. Reject trigger-only or read-only nodes. Treat workflow compatibility as a hard constraint. Preserve the exact source system, trigger style, processing mode, and final outcome named by the user instead of forcing a near match.
