2026-03-20 16:16:59,848 INFO n8n-assistant.trace: TRACE EVENT {"estimated_tokens": 1467, "event": "llm_prompt", "message_count": 2, "messages": [{"chars": 519, "content": "You are product_manager_agent for an n8n workflow assistant. Your role is abstract workflow planning only. Design how the workflow should behave stage by stage, but do not select node types, do not mention n8n nodes, do not mention credentials, do not mention parameter names, do not mention API endpoints, and do not generate workflow JSON. You are preparing a clean handoff for a future architect_agent that will later ground the plan into nodes. Preserve the user's exact semantics instead of broadening the request.", "role": "system"}, {"chars": 3835, "content": "Create an abstract workflow plan from this request context.\n\nOriginal workflow request: crea un workflow para coger los emails que vaya recibiendo y que los clasifique por nivel de urgencia con IA\nUse case title: crea un workflow para coger los emails que vaya recibiendo y que los clasifiq...\nBusiness problem: User requested a new workflow for: crea un workflow para coger los emails que vaya recibiendo y que los clasifique por nivel de urgencia con IA\nDesired outcome: Deliver an abstract workflow plan that satisfies: crea un workflow para coger los emails que vaya recibiendo y que los clasifique por nivel de urgencia con IA\nExpected business value: Provide a high-quality workflow plan ready for architectural grounding.\nClarifications already provided:\n-\n\nRules:\n- Output planning-level stages only.\n- If the request contains more than one explicit workflow operation, return at least 2 stages.\n- Otherwise return the minimum number of stages that still covers the full request coherently.\n- Each stage must define purpose, required_capabilities, expected_inputs, expected_outputs, dependencies, and success_criteria.\n- Each stage must have one dominant operable action that the workflow can actually execute.\n- Stages must describe behavior, not implementation details.\n- Do not mix source intake with downstream classification, storage, notification, or source mutation in the same stage.\n- User-visible outcomes like filtering, visibility, browsing, or 'seeing items in Gmail' belong in user_visible_goal or success_criteria, not as the primary action of a stage.\n- Preserve the exact source system, trigger style, processing mode, and final outcome named by the user.\n- Determine whether the workflow ends in a useful business effect, such as applying, saving, notifying, routing to a concrete downstream action, or intentionally exposing the result to the user.\n- If the workflow would otherwise end only in a classification, decision, routing outcome, or transformation result, ask what should happen with that result unless the user explicitly wants analysis or reporting only.\n- If the user said heuristic, rule-based, deterministic, manual, inbound, outgoing, receive, or send, keep that distinction explicit in the plan.\n- Distinguish receiving email from sending email; they are not interchangeable.\n- explicit_user_operations must list the canonical workflow operations explicitly requested by the user.\n- covered_operations must list the canonical workflow operations materially covered by the returned stages.\n- Do not leave requested operations covered only in workflow_summary, assumptions, data_flow, or handoff_notes; they must appear in real stages.\n- Do not invent validation, feedback, manual review, refinement, or approval stages unless the user explicitly asked for them.\n- Never output concrete node types or workflow JSON.\n- If the user explicitly named systems like Gmail, Slack, or Google Sheets, treat them as business/system context, not node choices.\n- Preserve concrete business constraints mentioned by the user, including label values, fallback values, source-system visibility requirements, trigger cadence, and whether classification should be semantic/AI-driven.\n- Carry those concrete constraints into the relevant stages and handoff_notes so architect_agent and engineer_agent can implement them later without re-asking the same thing.\n- Set planning_ready=true only if the abstract workflow plan is coherent enough to hand off to architect_agent.\n- If essential planning information is missing, set planning_ready=false and list the missing_information as concise user-facing clarification questions.\n- Write missing_information questions in the same language as the user's request.\n- handoff_notes must explain what architect_agent should preserve when grounding this plan into nodes.\n", "role": "user"}], "model": "local-model", "params": {"structured": true, "temperature": 0.2}, "request_id": "21a9816c53", "stage": "multi_agent.product_manager.abstract_plan", "total_chars": 4354, "ts_utc": "2026-03-20T15:16:59.848501+00:00"}
2026-03-20 16:17:19,127 INFO n8n-assistant.trace: TRACE EVENT {"content_chars": 3678, "content_preview": "{\"workflow_summary\":\"Abstract workflow plan for receiving emails and classifying them by urgency level using AI.\",\"stages\":[{\"id\":\"stage_1\",\"name\":\"Receive Emails\",\"purpose\":\"Capture incoming emails as they arrive in the user's email system (e.g., Gmail).\",\"stage_kind\":\"trigger_intake\",\"business_effect\":\"None; this stage only prepares data for downstream processing.\",\"target_entity\":\"Emails\",\"user_visible_goal\":\"The user should see that emails are being collected without interruption or loss.\",\"unresolved_decisions\":[],\"required_capabilities\":[\"Monitor an email inbox for new messages matching the workflow’s scope (e.g., all emails, specific labels, or addresses).\",\"Preserve full email content, headers, and metadata intact.\"],\"expected_inputs\":[\"Newly arrived emails that match the workflow’s intake criteria (e.g., sent to a monitored address).\"],\"expected_outputs\":[\"A stream of raw email data ready for urgency classification.\"],\"dependencies\":[],\"success_criteria\":[\"Every email that meets the intake criteria is captured exactly once and forwarded without loss or alteration.\",\"No duplicate emails are processed in this stage.\"],\"notes\":\"This stage must respect the user’s trigger style (e.g., real-time, batch) and source system (e.g., Gmail).\"},{\"id\":\"stage_2\",\"name\":\"Classify Email Urgency with AI\",\"purpose\":\"Analyze each email using an AI model to assign a discrete urgency level (e.g., Low, Medium, High, Critical).\",\"stage_kind\":\"transform_process\",\"business_effect\":\"None; this stage only produces a classification result.\",\"target_entity\":\"Emails\",\"user_visible_goal\":\"The user should be able to review the urgency labels assigned to their emails.\",\"unresolved_decisions\":[],\"required_capabilities\":[\"Apply an AI model that interprets email content, subject lines, sender reputation, and other contextual cues to output one of four predefined urgency levels: Low, Medium, High, Critical.\"],\"expected_inputs\":[\"Raw email data from Stage 1 (Receive Emails).\"],\"expected_outputs\":[\"Each email annotated with its assigned urgency level (Low, Medium, High, Critical) and confidence score if applicable.\"],\"dependencies\":[\"Stage 1 (Receive Emails)\"],\"success_criteria\":[\"Every input email receives exactly one urgency label.\",\"The AI model’s output is deterministic for the same input under identical conditions.\",\"Confidence scores, if produced, are meaningful and can be used for downstream filtering or review.\"],\"notes\":\"The concrete label values (Low, Medium, High, Critical) must be preserved. The architect_agent should ensure the AI model is grounded correctly in this stage.\"}],\"data_flow\":[{\"source_stage_id\":\"stage_1\",\"target_stage_id\":\"stage_2\",\"data_items\":[\"Raw email data\"],\"notes\":\"Data flows directly from intake to classification without intermediate storage or mutation.\"}],\"assumptions\":[\"The user intends for the workflow to run continuously, capturing emails as they arrive.\",\"The AI model is already available and does not require training or fine-tuning in this scope.\",\"No manual review or override of urgency labels is requested at this time.\"],\"missing_information\":[],\"handoff_notes\":[\"architect_agent must ground the trigger style (real-time vs. batch) and source system (e.g., Gmail) into concrete node choices in Stage 1.\",\"The AI model’s input/output schema, confidence scoring, and label set (Low, Medium, High, Critical) must be preserved exactly in Stage 2.\",\"No user-facing validation or feedback loop is required unless the user explicitly requests it later.\"],\"explicit_user_operations\":[\"Receive emails\",\"Classify urgency with AI\"],\"covered_operations\":[\"Receive emails\",\"Classify urgency with AI\"],\"planning_ready\":true}", "event": "llm_output", "model": "local-model", "request_id": "21a9816c53", "stage": "multi_agent.product_manager.abstract_plan", "structured": true, "ts_utc": "2026-03-20T15:17:19.127880+00:00"}
